WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.085 --> 00:00:02.610
- [Narrator] China has the
largest high-speed rail network

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:03.443
in the world,

00:00:03.443 --> 00:00:06.150
spanning over 40,000 kilometers.

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:08.610
And of the 10 fastest trains in the world,

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:10.410
four are in China.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:12.720
And China's busiest high-speed train,

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:17.670
the Beijing-Shanghai Rail,
runs 217 miles per hour.

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:20.760
The US, however, tells
a much different story.

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:25.590
Its high-speed rail system
spans 735 kilometers,

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:29.010
and its fastest train,
Amtrak's Acela Express,

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:31.950
goes just 150 miles per hour

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:35.040
and only for a few minutes
on part of the track.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:38.190
Since 2008, China has rapidly built up

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:39.930
its high-speed rail network.

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:41.460
The US in that time

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:43.470
has had several high-speed rail projects

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:45.300
shelved or delayed.

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.850
Here's how the US has fallen behind China

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:49.533
in high-speed rail technology.

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:53.280
One key difference
between the US and China

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:55.740
has to do with the
tracks the trains run on.

00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:58.500
- In the case of the US, as we can see,

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:00.900
a lot of the Northeast Corridors

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:04.350
are based on hundreds
years of the old tracks.

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:07.650
There are also tunnels and
the curvatures and bridges

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:09.450
that are really owed

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:12.090
that cannot really allow the trains to run

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:13.950
at the faster speed.

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:15.810
- [Narrator] These tracks
were originally built

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:17.730
for freight and passenger trains

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:19.500
to use at the same time

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:22.980
so they have many points of
intersections and crossings,

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:25.830
and the Acela runs on this track.

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:29.010
But China has tracks that are
dedicated to high-speed rail.

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:33.377
- They are really required to
have a more smoother curves,

00:01:33.377 --> 00:01:38.377
also they needed to have
more slower gradients,

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:43.260
and so that allow the trains
to run smoothly and safely.

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:45.060
- [Narrator] One of the
reasons trains in America

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:46.590
can't reach their top speeds

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:48.600
is because of how the tracks are designed.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:51.690
- Even though the trains are
capable for Amtrak to run 186,

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:54.090
they'll never see more
than 160 miles per hour

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:56.580
because the infrastructure
is still being developed

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:57.870
and isn't quite there yet.

00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:00.690
- [Narrator] It comes down
to curves on the tracks.

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:03.300
Many conventional
passenger trains in the US

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:06.420
only operate 10 to 15
miles per hour faster

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:09.390
than freight trains on the
curved portion of tracks.

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:12.990
But China built its high-speed
rail on elevated tracks,

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:15.570
traveling on what's called viaducts.

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:17.070
- So the tolerances in high-speed

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:19.710
are pretty precise,
pretty tightly defined,

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:21.930
and so the track and overhead
wires need to be designed

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:24.390
in a way to be able to
allow those higher speeds.

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:25.470
- [Narrator] There's also a difference

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:27.240
in where stations are located.

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:29.400
In China, stations for high-speed rails

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:33.360
are located in both suburban
and rural areas of the country,

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:34.830
whereas in the US,

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:37.710
stations are primarily
located in urban centers

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:40.890
like New York City,
Boston, and Washington, DC.

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:42.840
- When you're looking to identify

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:44.760
where a high-speed line should go,

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:47.760
it really starts with where
are the two population centers

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:49.140
you're trying to connect.

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:53.490
And the magic formula is
really find two city pairs

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:56.790
that have a minimum of 3
million people on either side

00:02:56.790 --> 00:02:59.100
and a distance of about two to 500 miles.

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:01.080
- In order to achieve a higher speed,

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:03.540
definitely, a dedicated
passenger rail system

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:05.400
is more needed.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:06.330
- [Narrator] Despite the fact

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:09.300
that the US has been slow
to develop high-speed rail,

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:11.460
railroads actually used to dominate

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:13.080
interstate transportation,

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:15.600
but that shifted during
the mid-20th century

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:18.030
when the Interstate
Highway System was born.

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:19.830
Since then, American history

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:21.990
has been littered with
high-speed rail plans

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:23.550
that never really took off.

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:27.990
- Automobile and air travel and car travel

00:03:27.990 --> 00:03:31.770
has essentially become the
major modes of travel in the US.

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:34.380
To make that kind of behavior change,

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:37.080
that basically needs a lot of efforts

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:39.300
to help people understand

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:41.700
why this is a need to have another mode

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:43.560
and what are the benefits of that.

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:45.150
- [Narrator] China's
high-speed rail, however,

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:47.850
consistently had backing
from the government,

00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:50.190
investing billions of dollars,

00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:52.950
including initial partners
with foreign firms.

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:55.110
Contracts were awarded on the condition

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:57.300
that foreign companies assemble the trains

00:03:57.300 --> 00:03:59.760
and also train Chinese engineers.

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:02.310
- So they're trying to understand

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:04.110
that the foreign technologies,

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:04.943
how it works,

00:04:04.943 --> 00:04:08.190
but they're also trying to
make for certain innovations

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:09.450
on top of that.

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:10.950
- [Narrator] Eventually China developed

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:15.090
its own train manufacturing
company, the CRRC.

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:16.680
And now the CRRC

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:19.710
is one of the largest train
manufacturers in the world.

00:04:19.710 --> 00:04:20.760
- In the case of China,

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:23.940
because it's a central government system,

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:27.074
so large infrastructure projects
like high-speed rail system

00:04:27.074 --> 00:04:29.280
can be really developed

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:32.340
based on the kind of consistent plant.

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:34.590
- [Narrator] The US has
also had foreign companies

00:04:34.590 --> 00:04:37.650
come to the country to develop
a high-speed rail network,

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:40.230
but it's been met with several challenges.

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:42.180
Take for example, Illinois.

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:45.600
Back in 2019, Nippon Sharyo of Japan

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:48.300
tried to build a high-speed
rail in the states.

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:51.750
However, the company struggled
to adapt designs to the US

00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:54.960
and faced delays in getting new rail cars.

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:58.230
And changing administrations doesn't help.

00:04:58.230 --> 00:05:00.750
California approved a plan in 2008

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:02.550
to build a high-speed rail network,

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:04.170
but the project has been delayed

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:07.560
because of a lack of funding
and inconsistent support.

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:09.300
- The train technology is already here.

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:10.980
What we're seeing right now in the US

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:12.540
at this specific moment in time

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:14.040
is not that dissimilar

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:17.010
to what was happening
in Europe in the 1970s

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:19.050
where there was initially a lot of concern

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:21.480
or objection about trying
to do something new.

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:22.766
And it wasn't until the first system

00:05:22.766 --> 00:05:24.300
started to be put in place

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:25.935
and people saw the real promise

00:05:25.935 --> 00:05:27.759
that high-speed was able to deliver on.

00:05:27.759 --> 00:05:29.070
And then once the political

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:30.930
and public support was behind it,

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:32.841
it really took off and grew.

00:05:32.841 --> 00:05:35.424
(gentle music)

